+# FAQ for newcomers
+
+## How do I install the command line tool?
+
+You need Python 3.11 or newer. Install it with `pipx install tally-toolkit`, then check it with `tallyctl version`.
+If the command is not found, run `pipx ensurepath` and open a new terminal.
+
+## What is the tool called now?
+
+`tallyctl`. It was called `tally-cli` until the 2026-09 release; the old name still works as an alias for one more
+release and will then be removed.
+
+## Which port does tally listen on?
+
+Port 8731. The old port 8080 is closed, so anything still pointing at it gets a refused connection.
+
+## How do I clear the cache?
+
+Run `tallyctl cache clear`. It clears all regions; add `--region NAME` to clear only one. The old purge command has
+been removed.
+
+## How long until an unanswered page escalates?
+
+15 minutes without acknowledgement. The page goes from the primary on-call to the secondary, then to the engineering
+manager.
+
+## Does the pager wake me at night?
+
+Only for severity 1: customer-facing data loss or a full outage. Between 22:00 and 07:00, severity 2 incidents do not
+page; they wait for business hours. This does not mean you can ignore the dashboards.
+
+## What do I do when the ingest queue is stuck?
+
+Run `tallyctl restart ingest --drain` first. It finishes in-flight work, parks the stuck batch in the dead-letter table
+and restarts the consumer clean. A plain restart does not help. Look for the cause afterwards.
+
+## What do I do when the API certificate has expired?
+
+Renew it with `tallyctl certs renew api`, then restart the API with `tallyctl restart api`. The new certificate is only
+served after the restart, so do not skip it. `tallyctl certs list` shows the expiry dates.
